<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>


     Our per-share book value increased 14.3% during 1993.  Over=20
the last 29 years (that is, since present management took over)=20
book value has grown from $19 to $8,854, or at a rate of 23.3%=20
compounded annually.

     During the year, Berkshire's net worth increased by $1.5=20
billion, a figure affected by two negative and two positive non-
operating items.  For the sake of completeness, I'll explain them=20
here.  If you aren't thrilled by accounting, however, feel free=20
to fast-forward through this discussion:

     1.     The first negative was produced by a change in=20
            Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP)=20
            having to do with the taxes we accrue against=20
            unrealized appreciation in the securities we=20
            carry at market value.  The old rule said that=20
            the tax rate used should be the one in effect=20
            when the appreciation took place.  Therefore,=20
            at the end of 1992, we were using a rate of 34%=20
            on the $6.4 billion of gains generated after=20
            1986 and 28% on the $1.2 billion of gains=20
            generated before that.  The new rule stipulates=20
            that the current tax rate should be applied to=20
            all gains.  The rate in the first quarter of=20
            1993, when this rule went into effect, was 34%.=20
            Applying that rate to our pre-1987 gains=20
            reduced net worth by $70 million.

     2.     The second negative, related to the first, came=20
            about because the corporate tax rate was raised=20
            in the third quarter of 1993 to 35%.  This=20
            change required us to make an additional charge=20
            of 1% against all of our unrealized gains, and=20
            that charge penalized net worth by $75 million.=20
            Oddly, GAAP required both this charge and the=20
            one described above to be deducted from the=20
            earnings we report, even though the unrealized=20
            appreciation that gave rise to the charges was=20
            never included in earnings, but rather was=20
            credited directly to net worth.

     3.     Another 1993 change in GAAP affects the value=20
            at which we carry the securities that we own. =20
            In recent years, both the common stocks and=20
            certain common-equivalent securities held by=20
            our insurance companies have been valued at=20
            market, whereas equities held by our non-
            insurance subsidiaries or by the parent company=20
            were carried at their aggregate cost or market,=20
            whichever was lower.  Now GAAP says that <i>all</i>=20
            common stocks should be carried at market, a=20
            rule we began following in the fourth quarter=20
            of 1993.  This change produced a gain in=20
            Berkshire's reported net worth of about $172=20
            million.

     4.     Finally, we issued some stock last year.  In a=20
            transaction described in last year's Annual=20
            Report, we issued 3,944 shares in early=20
            January, 1993 upon the conversion of $46=20
            million convertible debentures that we had=20
            called for redemption.  Additionally, we issued=20
            25,203 shares when we acquired Dexter Shoe, a=20
            purchase discussed later in this report.  The=20
            overall result was that our shares outstanding=20
            increased by 29,147 and our net worth by about=20
            $478 million.  Per-share book value also grew,=20
            because the shares issued in these transactions=20
            carried a price above their book value.

     Of course, it's per-share intrinsic value, not book value,=20
that counts.  Book value is an accounting term that measures the=20
capital, including retained earnings, that has been put into a=20
business.  Intrinsic value is a present-value estimate of the=20
cash that can be taken out of a business during its remaining=20
life.  At most companies, the two values are unrelated. =20
Berkshire, however, is an exception:  Our book value, though=20
significantly below our intrinsic value, serves as a useful=20
device for tracking that key figure.  In 1993, each measure grew=20
by roughly 14%, advances that I would call satisfactory but=20
unexciting.

     These gains, however, were outstripped by a much larger gain=20
- 39% - in Berkshire's market price.  Over time, of course,=20
market price and intrinsic value will arrive at about the same=20
destination.  But in the short run the two often diverge in a=20
major way, a phenomenon I've discussed in the past.  Two years=20
ago, Coca-Cola and Gillette, both large holdings of ours, enjoyed=20
market price increases that dramatically outpaced their earnings=20
gains.  In the 1991 Annual Report, I said that the stocks of=20
these companies could not continuously overperform their=20
businesses.

     From 1991 to 1993, Coke and Gillette increased their annual=20
operating earnings per share by 38% and 37% respectively, but=20
their market prices moved up only 11% and 6%.  In other words,=20
the companies overperformed their stocks, a result that no doubt=20
partly reflects Wall Street's new apprehension about brand names.=20
Whatever the reason, what will count over time is the earnings=20
performance of these companies.  If they prosper, Berkshire will=20
also prosper, though not in a lock-step manner.

     Let me add a lesson from history:  Coke went public in 1919=20
at $40 per share.  By the end of 1920 the market, coldly=20
reevaluating Coke's future prospects, had battered the stock down=20
by more than 50%, to $19.50.  At yearend 1993, that single share,=20
with dividends reinvested, was worth more than $2.1 million.  As=20
Ben Graham said:  "In the short-run, the market is a voting=20
machine - reflecting a voter-registration test that requires only=20
money, not intelligence or emotional stability - but in the long-
run, the market is a weighing machine."

     So how should Berkshire's over-performance in the market=20
last year be viewed?  Clearly, Berkshire was selling at a higher=20
percentage of intrinsic value at the end of 1993 than was the=20
case at the beginning of the year.  On the other hand, in a world=20
of 6% or 7% long-term interest rates, Berkshire's market price=20
was not inappropriate if - and you should understand that this is=20
a huge if - Charlie Munger, Berkshire's Vice Chairman, and I can=20
attain our long-standing goal of increasing Berkshire's per-share=20
intrinsic value at an average annual rate of 15%.  We have not=20
retreated from this goal.  But we again emphasize, as we have for=20
many years, that the growth in our capital base makes 15% an=20
ever-more difficult target to hit.

     What we have going for us is a growing collection of good-
sized operating businesses that possess economic characteristics=20
ranging from good to terrific, run by managers whose performance=20
ranges from terrific to terrific.  You need have no worries about=20
this group.

     The capital-allocation work that Charlie and I do at the=20
parent company, using the funds that our managers deliver to us,=20
has a less certain outcome:  It is not easy to find new=20
businesses and managers comparable to those we have.  Despite=20
that difficulty, Charlie and I relish the search, and we are=20
happy to report an important success in 1993.

<b>Dexter Shoe</b>

     What we did last year was build on our 1991 purchase of H.=20
H. Brown, a superbly-run manufacturer of work shoes, boots and=20
other footwear.  Brown has been a real winner:  Though we had=20
high hopes to begin with, these expectations have been=20
considerably exceeded thanks to Frank Rooney, Jim Issler and the=20
talented managers who work with them.  Because of our confidence=20
in Frank's team, we next acquired Lowell Shoe, at the end of=20
1992.  Lowell was a long-established manufacturer of women's and=20
nurses' shoes, but its business needed some fixing.  Again,=20
results have surpassed our expectations.  So we promptly jumped=20
at the chance last year to acquire Dexter Shoe of Dexter, Maine,=20
which manufactures popular-priced men's and women's shoes. =20
Dexter, I can assure you, needs <i>no</i> fixing:  It is one of the=20
best-managed companies Charlie and I have seen in our business=20
lifetimes.

     Harold Alfond, who started working in a shoe factory at 25=20
cents an hour when he was 20, founded Dexter in 1956 with $10,000=20
of capital.  He was joined in 1958 by Peter Lunder, his nephew. =20
The two of them have since built a business that now produces over=20
7.5 million pairs of shoes annually, most of them made in Maine=20
and the balance in Puerto Rico.  As you probably know, the=20
domestic shoe industry is generally thought to be unable to=20
compete with imports from low-wage countries.  But someone forgot=20
to tell this to the ingenious managements of Dexter and H. H.=20
Brown and to their skilled labor forces, which together make the=20
U.S. plants of both companies highly competitive against all=20
comers.

     Dexter's business includes 77 retail outlets, located=20
primarily in the Northeast.  The company is also a major=20
manufacturer of golf shoes, producing about 15% of U.S. output. =20
Its bread and butter, though, is the manufacture of traditional=20
shoes for traditional retailers, a job at which it excels:  Last=20
year both Nordstrom and J.C. Penney bestowed special awards upon=20
Dexter for its performance as a supplier during 1992.

     Our 1993 results include Dexter only from our date of=20
merger, November 7th.  In 1994, we expect Berkshire's shoe=20
operations to have more than $550 million in sales, and we would=20
not be surprised if the combined pre-tax earnings of these=20
businesses topped $85 million.  Five years ago we had no thought=20
of getting into shoes.  Now we have 7,200 employees in that=20
industry, and I sing "There's No Business Like Shoe Business" as=20
I drive to work.  So much for strategic plans.

     At Berkshire, we have no view of the future that dictates=20
what businesses or industries we will enter.  Indeed, we think=20
it's usually poison for a corporate giant's shareholders if it=20
embarks upon new ventures pursuant to some grand vision.  We=20
prefer instead to focus on the economic characteristics of=20
businesses that we wish to own and the personal characteristics=20
of managers with whom we wish to associate - and then to hope we=20
get lucky in finding the two in combination.  At Dexter, we did.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     And now we pause for a short commercial:  Though they owned=20
a business jewel, we believe that Harold and Peter (who were not=20
interested in cash) made a sound decision in exchanging their=20
Dexter stock for shares of Berkshire.  What they did, in effect,=20
was trade a 100% interest in a single terrific business for a=20
smaller interest in a large group of terrific businesses.  They=20
incurred no tax on this exchange and now own a security that can=20
be easily used for charitable or personal gifts, or that can be=20
converted to cash in amounts, and at times, of their own=20
choosing.  Should members of their families desire to, they can=20
pursue varying financial paths without running into the=20
complications that often arise when assets are concentrated in a=20
private business.

     For tax and other reasons, private companies also often find=20
it difficult to diversify outside their industries.  Berkshire,=20
in contrast, can diversify with ease.  So in shifting their=20
ownership to Berkshire, Dexter's shareholders solved a=20
reinvestment problem.  Moreover, though Harold and Peter now have=20
non-controlling shares in Berkshire, rather than controlling=20
shares in Dexter, they know they will be treated as partners and=20
that we will follow owner-oriented practices.  If they elect to=20
retain their Berkshire shares, their investment result from the=20
merger date forward will exactly parallel my own result.  Since I=20
have a huge percentage of my net worth committed for life to=20
Berkshire shares - and since the company will issue me neither=20
restricted shares nor stock options - my gain-loss equation will=20
always match that of all other owners.

     Additionally, Harold and Peter know that at Berkshire we can=20
keep our promises:  There will be no changes of control or=20
culture at Berkshire for many decades to come.  Finally, and of=20
paramount importance, Harold and Peter can be sure that they will=20
get to run their business - an activity they dearly love -=20
exactly as they did before the merger.  At Berkshire, we do not=20
tell .400 hitters how to swing.

     What made sense for Harold and Peter probably makes sense=20
for a few other owners of large private businesses.  So, if you=20
have a business that might fit, let me hear from you.  Our=20
acquisition criteria are set forth in the appendix on page 22.

