     A large amount of information about these businesses is given=20
on pages 38-49, where you will also find our segment earnings=20
reported on a GAAP basis.  In addition, on pages 52-59, we have=20
rearranged Berkshire's financial data into four segments on a non-
GAAP basis, a presentation that corresponds to the way Charlie and=20
I think about the company.  Our intent is to supply you with the=20
financial information that we would wish you to give us if our=20
positions were reversed.

<b>"Look-Through" Earnings</b>

     We've previously discussed look-through earnings, which we=20
believe more accurately portray the earnings of Berkshire than does=20
our GAAP result.  As we calculate them, look-through earnings=20
consist of: (1) the operating earnings reported in the previous=20
section, plus; (2) the retained operating earnings of major=20
investees that, under GAAP accounting, are not reflected in our=20
profits, less; (3) an allowance for the tax that would be paid by=20
Berkshire if these retained earnings of investees had instead been=20
distributed to us.  The "operating earnings" of which we speak here=20
exclude capital gains, special accounting items and major=20
restructuring charges.

     Over time, our look-through earnings need to increase at about=20
15% annually if our intrinsic value is to grow at that rate.  Last=20
year, I explained that we had to increase these earnings to about=20
$1.8 billion in the year 2000, were we to meet the 15% goal. =20
Because we issued additional shares in 1993, the amount needed has=20
risen to about $1.85 billion.

     That is a tough goal, but one that we expect you to hold us=20
to.  In the past, we've criticized the managerial practice of=20
shooting the arrow of performance and <i>then</i> painting the target,=20
centering it on whatever point the arrow happened to hit.  We will=20
instead risk embarrassment by painting first and shooting later.

     If we are to hit the bull's-eye, we will need markets that=20
allow the purchase of businesses and securities on sensible terms.=20
Right now, markets are difficult, but they can - and will - change=20
in unexpected ways and at unexpected times.  In the meantime, we'll=20
try to resist the temptation to do something marginal simply=20
because we are long on cash.  There's no use running if you're on=20
the wrong road.

