     We have told you that we expect the undistributed,=20
hypothetically-taxed earnings of our investees to produce at least=20
equivalent gains in Berkshire's intrinsic value.  To date, we have=20
far exceeded that expectation.  For example, in 1986 we bought=20
three million shares of Capital Cities/ABC for $172.50 per share=20
and late last year sold one-third of that holding for $630 per=20
share.  After paying 35% capital gains taxes, we realized a $297=20
million profit from the sale.  In contrast, during the eight years=20
we held these shares, the retained earnings of Cap Cities=20
attributable to them - hypothetically taxed at a lower 14% in=20
accordance with our look-through method - were only $152 million. =20
In other words, we paid a much larger tax bill than our look-
through presentations to you have assumed and nonetheless realized=20
a gain that far exceeded the undistributed earnings allocable to=20
these shares.

     We expect such pleasant outcomes to recur often in the future=20
and therefore believe our look-through earnings to be a=20
conservative representation of Berkshire's true economic earnings.

<b>Taxes</b>

     As our Cap Cities sale emphasizes, Berkshire is a substantial=20
payer of federal income taxes.  In aggregate, we will pay 1993=20
federal income taxes of $390 million, about $200 million of that=20
attributable to operating earnings and $190 million to realized=20
capital gains.  Furthermore, our share of the 1993 federal and=20
foreign income taxes paid by our investees is well over $400=20
million, a figure you don't see on our financial statements but=20
that is nonetheless real.  Directly and indirectly, Berkshire's=20
1993 federal income tax payments will be about 1/2 of 1% of the total=20
paid last year by all American corporations.

     Speaking for our own shares, Charlie and I have absolutely no=20
complaint about these taxes.  We know we work in a market-based=20
economy that rewards our efforts far more bountifully than it does=20
the efforts of others whose output is of equal or greater benefit=20
to society.  Taxation should, and does, partially redress this=20
inequity.  But we still remain extraordinarily well-treated.

     Berkshire and its shareholders, in combination, would pay a=20
much smaller tax if Berkshire operated as a partnership or "S"=20
corporation, two structures often used for business activities. =20
For a variety of reasons, that's not feasible for Berkshire to do.=20
However, the penalty our corporate form imposes is mitigated -=20
though far from eliminated - by our strategy of investing for the=20
long term.  Charlie and I would follow a buy-and-hold policy even=20
if we ran a tax-exempt institution.  We think it the soundest way=20
to invest, and it also goes down the grain of our personalities.  A=20
third reason to favor this policy, however, is the fact that taxes=20
are due only when gains are realized.

     Through my favorite comic strip, Li'l Abner, I got a chance=20
during my youth to see the benefits of delayed taxes, though I=20
missed the lesson at the time.  Making his readers feel superior,=20
Li'l Abner bungled happily, but moronically, through life in=20
Dogpatch.  At one point he became infatuated with a New York=20
temptress, Appassionatta Van Climax, but despaired of marrying her=20
because he had only a single silver dollar and she was interested=20
solely in millionaires.  Dejected, Abner took his problem to Old=20
Man Mose, the font of all knowledge in Dogpatch.  Said the sage: =20
Double your money 20 times and Appassionatta will be yours (1, 2,=20
4, 8 . . . . 1,048,576).

     My last memory of the strip is Abner entering a roadhouse,=20
dropping his dollar into a slot machine, and hitting a jackpot that=20
spilled money all over the floor.  Meticulously following Mose's=20
advice, Abner picked up two dollars and went off to find his next=20
double.  Whereupon I dumped Abner and began reading Ben Graham.

     Mose clearly was overrated as a guru:  Besides failing to=20
anticipate Abner's slavish obedience to instructions, he also=20
forgot about taxes.  Had Abner been subject, say, to the 35%=20
federal tax rate that Berkshire pays, and had he managed one double=20
annually, he would after 20 years only have accumulated $22,370. =20
Indeed, had he kept on both getting his annual doubles and paying a=20
35% tax on each, he would have needed 7 1/2 years more to reach the=20
$1 million required to win Appassionatta.

     But what if Abner had instead put his dollar in a single=20
investment and held it until it doubled the same 27 1/2 times?  In=20
that case, he would have realized about $200 million pre-tax or,=20
after paying a $70 million tax in the final year, about $130=20
million after-tax.  For that, Appassionatta would have crawled to=20
Dogpatch.  Of course, with 27 1/2 years having passed, how=20
Appassionatta would have looked to a fellow sitting on $130 million=20
is another question.

     What this little tale tells us is that tax-paying investors=20
will realize a far, far greater sum from a single investment that=20
compounds internally at a given rate than from a succession of=20
investments compounding at the same rate.  But I suspect many=20
Berkshire shareholders figured that out long ago.

<b>Insurance Operations</b>

     At this point in the report we've customarily provided you=20
with a table showing the annual "combined ratio" of the insurance=20
industry for the preceding decade.  This ratio compares total=20
insurance costs (losses incurred plus expenses) to revenue from=20
premiums.  For many years, the ratio has been above 100, a level=20
indicating an underwriting loss.  That is, the industry has taken=20
in less money each year from its policyholders than it has had to=20
pay for operating expenses and for loss events that occurred during=20
the year.

     Offsetting this grim equation is a happier fact:  Insurers get=20
to hold on to their policyholders' money for a time before paying=20
it out.  This happens because most policies require that premiums=20
be prepaid and, more importantly, because it often takes time to=20
resolve loss claims.  Indeed, in the case of certain lines of=20
insurance, such as product liability or professional malpractice,=20
many years may elapse between the loss event and payment.

     To oversimplify the matter somewhat, the total of the funds=20
prepaid by policyholders and the funds earmarked for incurred-but-
not-yet-paid claims is called "the float." In the past, the=20
industry was able to suffer a combined ratio of 107 to 111 and=20
still break even from its insurance writings because of the=20
earnings derived from investing this float.

     As interest rates have fallen, however, the value of float has=20
substantially declined.  Therefore, the data that we have provided=20
in the past are no longer useful for year-to-year comparisons of=20
industry profitability.  A company writing at the same combined=20
ratio now as in the 1980's today has a far less attractive business=20
than it did then.

     Only by making an analysis that incorporates both underwriting=20
results and the current risk-free earnings obtainable from float=20
can one evaluate the true economics of the business that a=20
property-casualty insurer writes.  Of course, the <i>actual</i> investment=
=20
results that an insurer achieves from the use of both float and=20
stockholders' funds is also of major importance and should be=20
carefully examined when an investor is assessing managerial=20
performance.  But that should be a separate analysis from the one=20
we are discussing here.  The value of float funds - in effect,=20
their transfer price as they move from the insurance operation to=20
the investment operation - should be determined simply by the risk-
free, long-term rate of interest.

