     As you can see, in our insurance operation last year we had=20
the use of $2.6 billion at no cost; in fact we were paid $31=20
million, our underwriting profit, to hold these funds.  This sounds=20
good  - is good - but is far from as good as it sounds.

     We temper our enthusiasm because we write a large volume of=20
"super-cat" policies (which other insurance and reinsurance=20
companies buy to recover part of the losses they suffer from mega-
catastrophes) and because last year we had no losses of consequence=20
from this activity.  As that suggests, the truly catastrophic=20
Midwestern floods of 1993 did not trigger super-cat losses, the=20
reason being that very few flood policies are purchased from=20
private insurers.

     It would be fallacious, however, to conclude from this single-
year result that the super-cat business is a wonderful one, or even=20
a satisfactory one.  A simple example will illustrate the fallacy:=20
Suppose there is an event that occurs 25 times in every century. =20
If you annually give 5-for-1 odds against its occurrence <i>that</i> year,=
=20
you will have many more winning years than losers.  Indeed, you may=20
go a straight six, seven or more years without loss.  You also will=20
eventually go broke.

     At Berkshire, we naturally believe we are obtaining adequate=20
premiums and giving more like 3 1/2-for-1 odds.  But there is no way=20
for us - or anyone else - to calculate the true odds on super-cat=20
coverages.  In fact, it will take decades for us to find out=20
whether our underwriting judgment has been sound.

     What we do know is that when a loss comes, it's likely to be a=20
lulu.  There may well be years when Berkshire will suffer losses=20
from the super-cat business equal to three or four times what we=20
earned from it in 1993.  When Hurricane Andrew blew in 1992, we=20
paid out about $125 million.  Because we've since expanded our=20
super-cat business, a similar storm today could cost us $600=20
million.

     So far, we have been lucky in 1994.  As I write this letter,=20
we are estimating that our losses from the Los Angeles earthquake=20
will be nominal.  But if the quake had been a 7.5 instead of a 6.8,=20
it would have been a different story.

     Berkshire is ideally positioned to write super-cat policies. =20
In Ajit Jain, we have by far the best manager in this business. =20
Additionally, companies writing these policies need enormous=20
capital, and our net worth is ten to twenty times larger than that=20
of our main competitors.  In most lines of insurance, huge=20
resources aren't that important:  An insurer can diversify the=20
risks it writes and, if necessary, can lay off risks to reduce=20
concentration in its portfolio.  That isn't possible in the super-
cat business.  So these competitors are forced into offering far=20
smaller limits than those we can provide.  Were they bolder, they=20
would run the risk that a mega-catastrophe - or a confluence of=20
smaller catastrophes - would wipe them out.

     One indication of our premier strength and reputation is that=20
each of the four largest reinsurance companies in the world buys=20
very significant reinsurance coverage from Berkshire.  Better than=20
anyone else, these giants understand that the test of a reinsurer=20
is its ability and willingness to pay losses under trying=20
circumstances, not its readiness to accept premiums when things=20
look rosy.

     One caution:  There has recently been a substantial increase=20
in reinsurance capacity.  Close to $5 billion of equity capital has=20
been raised by reinsurers, almost all of them newly-formed=20
entities.  Naturally these new entrants are hungry to write=20
business so that they can justify the projections they utilized in=20
attracting capital.  This new competition won't affect our 1994=20
operations; we're filled up there, primarily with business written=20
in 1993.  But we are now seeing signs of price deterioration.  If=20
this trend continues, we will resign ourselves to much-reduced=20
volume, keeping ourselves available, though, for the large,=20
sophisticated buyer who requires a super-cat insurer with large=20
capacity and a sure ability to pay losses.

     In other areas of our insurance business, our homestate=20
operation, led by Rod Eldred; our workers' compensation business,=20
headed by Brad Kinstler; our credit-card operation, managed by the=20
Kizer family; and National Indemnity's traditional auto and general=20
liability business, led by Don Wurster, all achieved excellent=20
results.  In combination, these four units produced a significant=20
underwriting profit and substantial float.

     All in all, we have a first-class insurance business.  Though=20
its results will be highly volatile, this operation possesses an=20
intrinsic value that exceeds its book value by a large amount -=20
larger, in fact, than is the case at any other Berkshire business.

