     Considering the similarity of this year's list and the last,=20
you may decide your management is hopelessly comatose.  But we=20
continue to think that it is usually foolish to part with an=20
interest in a business that is both understandable and durably=20
wonderful.  Business interests of that kind are simply too hard to=20
replace.

     Interestingly, corporate managers have no trouble=20
understanding that point when they are focusing on a business they=20
operate:  A parent company that owns a subsidiary with superb long-
term economics is not likely to sell that entity regardless of=20
price.  "Why," the CEO would ask, "should I part with my crown=20
jewel?"  Yet that same CEO, when it comes to running his personal=20
investment portfolio, will offhandedly - and even impetuously -=20
move from business to business when presented with no more than=20
superficial arguments by his broker for doing so.  The worst of=20
these is perhaps, "You can't go broke taking a profit."  Can you=20
imagine a CEO using this line to urge his board to sell a star=20
subsidiary?  In our view, what makes sense in business also makes=20
sense in stocks:  An investor should ordinarily hold a small piece=20
of an outstanding business with the same tenacity that an owner=20
would exhibit if he owned all of that business.

     Earlier I mentioned the financial results that could have been=20
achieved by investing $40 in The Coca-Cola Co. in 1919.  In 1938,=20
more than 50 years after the introduction of Coke, and long after=20
the drink was firmly established as an American icon, <i>Fortune</i> did=20
an excellent story on the company.  In the second paragraph the=20
writer reported:  "Several times every year a weighty and serious=20
investor looks long and with profound respect at Coca-Cola's=20
record, but comes regretfully to the conclusion that he is looking=20
too late.  The specters of saturation and competition rise before=20
him."

     Yes, competition there was in 1938 and in 1993 as well.  But=20
it's worth noting that in 1938 The Coca-Cola Co. sold 207 million=20
cases of soft drinks (if its gallonage then is converted into the=20
192-ounce cases used for measurement today) and in 1993 it sold=20
about 10.7 billion cases, a 50-fold increase in physical volume=20
from a company that in 1938 was already dominant in its very major=20
industry.  Nor was the party over in 1938 for an investor:  Though=20
the $40 invested in 1919 in one share had (with dividends=20
reinvested) turned into $3,277 by the end of 1938, a fresh $40 then=20
invested in Coca-Cola stock would have grown to $25,000 by yearend=20
1993.

     I can't resist one more quote from that 1938 <i>Fortune</i> story: =20
"It would be hard to name any company comparable in size to Coca-
Cola and selling, as Coca-Cola does, an unchanged product that can=20
point to a ten-year record anything like Coca-Cola's."  In the 55=20
years that have since passed, Coke's product line has broadened=20
somewhat, but it's remarkable how well that description still fits.

     Charlie and I decided long ago that in an investment lifetime=20
it's just too hard to make hundreds of smart decisions.  That=20
judgment became ever more compelling as Berkshire's capital=20
mushroomed and the universe of investments that could significantly=20
affect our results shrank dramatically.  Therefore, we adopted a=20
strategy that required our being smart - and not too smart at that=20
- only a very few times.  Indeed, we'll now settle for one good=20
idea a year.  (Charlie says it's my turn.)

     The strategy we've adopted precludes our following standard=20
diversification dogma.  Many pundits would therefore say the=20
strategy must be riskier than that employed by more conventional=20
investors.  We disagree.  We believe that a policy of portfolio=20
concentration may well <i>decrease</i> risk if it raises, as it should, =20
both the intensity with which an investor thinks about a business=20
and the comfort-level he must feel with its economic characteristics=20
before buying into it.  In stating this opinion, we define risk,=20
using dictionary terms, as "the possibility of loss or injury."

     Academics, however, like to define investment "risk"=20
differently, averring that it is the relative volatility of a stock=20
or portfolio of stocks - that is, their volatility as compared to=20
that of a large universe of stocks.  Employing data bases and=20
statistical skills, these academics compute with precision the=20
"beta" of a stock - its relative volatility in the past - and then=20
build arcane investment and capital-allocation theories around this=20
calculation.  In their hunger for a single statistic to measure=20
risk, however, they forget a fundamental principle:  It is better=20
to be approximately right than precisely wrong.

     For owners of a business - and that's the way we think of=20
shareholders - the academics' definition of risk is far off the=20
mark, so much so that it produces absurdities.  For example, under=20
beta-based theory, a stock that has dropped very sharply compared=20
to the market - as had Washington Post when we bought it in 1973 -=20
becomes "riskier" at the lower price than it was at the higher=20
price.  Would that description have then made any sense to someone=20
who was offered the entire company at a vastly-reduced price?

     In fact, the true investor <i>welcomes</i> volatility.  Ben Graham=20
explained why in Chapter 8 of <i>The Intelligent Investor.</i>  There he=20
introduced "Mr. Market," an obliging fellow who shows up every day=20
to either buy from you or sell to you, whichever you wish.  The=20
more manic-depressive this chap is, the greater the opportunities=20
available to the investor.  That's true because a wildly=20
fluctuating market means that irrationally low prices will=20
periodically be attached to solid businesses.  It is impossible to=20
see how the availability of such prices can be thought of as=20
increasing the hazards for an investor who is totally free to=20
either ignore the market or exploit its folly.

     In assessing risk, a beta purist will disdain examining what a=20
company produces, what its competitors are doing, or how much=20
borrowed money the business employs.  He may even prefer not to=20
know the company's name.  What he treasures is the price history of=20
its stock.  In contrast, we'll happily forgo knowing the price=20
history and instead will seek whatever information will further our=20
understanding of the company's business.  After we buy a stock,=20
consequently, we would not be disturbed if markets closed for a=20
year or two.  We don't need a daily quote on our 100% position in=20
See's or H. H. Brown to validate our well-being.  Why, then, should=20
we need a quote on our 7% interest in Coke?

     In our opinion, the real risk that an investor must assess is=20
whether his aggregate after-tax receipts from an investment=20
(including those he receives on sale) will, over his prospective=20
holding period, give him at least as much purchasing power as he=20
had to begin with, plus a modest rate of interest on that initial=20
stake.  Though this risk cannot be calculated with engineering=20
precision, it can in some cases be judged with a degree of accuracy=20
that is useful.  The primary factors bearing upon this evaluation=20
are:

     1) The certainty with which the long-term economic=20
        characteristics of the business can be evaluated;

     2) The certainty with which management can be evaluated,=20
        both as to its ability to realize the full potential of=20
        the business and to wisely employ its cash flows;

     3) The certainty with which management can be counted on=20
        to channel the rewards from the business to the=20
        shareholders rather than to itself;

     4) The purchase price of the business;

     5) The levels of taxation and inflation that will be=20
        experienced and that will determine the degree by which=20
        an investor's purchasing-power return is reduced from his=20
        gross return.

     These factors will probably strike many analysts as unbearably=20
fuzzy, since they cannot be extracted from a data base of any kind.=20
But the difficulty of precisely quantifying these matters does not=20
negate their importance nor is it insuperable.  Just as Justice=20
Stewart found it impossible to formulate a test for obscenity but=20
nevertheless asserted, "I know it when I see it," so also can=20
investors - in an inexact but useful way - "see" the risks inherent=20
in certain investments without reference to complex equations or=20
price histories.

     Is it really so difficult to conclude that Coca-Cola and=20
Gillette possess far less business risk over the long term than,=20
say, <i>any</i> computer company or retailer?  Worldwide, Coke sells about=
=20
44% of all soft drinks, and Gillette has more than a 60% share (in=20
value) of the blade market.  Leaving aside chewing gum, in which=20
Wrigley is dominant, I know of no other significant businesses in=20
which the leading company has long enjoyed such global power.

     Moreover, both Coke and Gillette have actually increased their=20
worldwide shares of market in recent years.  The might of their=20
brand names, the attributes of their products, and the strength of=20
their distribution systems give them an enormous competitive=20
advantage, setting up a protective moat around their economic=20
castles.  The average company, in contrast, does battle daily=20
without any such means of protection.  As Peter Lynch says, stocks=20
of companies selling commodity-like products should come with a=20
warning label:  "Competition may prove hazardous to human wealth."

	The competitive strengths of a Coke or Gillette are obvious to=20
even the casual observer of business.  Yet the beta of their stocks=20
is similar to that of a great many run-of-the-mill companies who=20
possess little or no competitive advantage.  Should we conclude=20
from this similarity that the competitive strength of Coke and=20
Gillette gains them nothing when business risk is being measured? =20
Or should we conclude that the risk in owning a piece of a company=20
- its stock - is somehow divorced from the long-term risk inherent=20
in its business operations?  We believe neither conclusion makes=20
sense and that equating beta with investment risk also makes no=20
sense.

     The theoretician bred on beta has no mechanism for=20
differentiating the risk inherent in, say, a single-product toy=20
company selling pet rocks or hula hoops from that of another toy=20
company whose sole product is Monopoly or Barbie.  But it's quite=20
possible for ordinary investors to make such distinctions if they=20
have a reasonable understanding of consumer behavior and the=20
factors that create long-term competitive strength or weakness. =20
Obviously, every investor will make mistakes.  But by confining=20
himself to a relatively few, easy-to-understand cases, a reasonably=20
intelligent, informed and diligent person can judge investment=20
risks with a useful degree of accuracy.

     In many industries, of course, Charlie and I can't determine=20
whether we are dealing with a "pet rock" or a "Barbie."  We=20
couldn't solve this problem, moreover, even if we were to spend=20
years intensely studying those industries.  Sometimes our own=20
intellectual shortcomings would stand in the way of understanding,=20
and in other cases the nature of the industry would be the=20
roadblock.  For example, a business that must deal with fast-moving=20
technology is not going to lend itself to reliable evaluations of=20
its long-term economics.  Did we foresee thirty years ago what=20
would transpire in the television-manufacturing or computer=20
industries?  Of course not.  (Nor did most of the investors and=20
corporate managers who enthusiastically entered those industries.)=20
Why, then, should Charlie and I now think we can predict the=20
future of other rapidly-evolving businesses?  We'll stick instead=20
with the easy cases.  Why search for a needle buried in a haystack=20
when one is sitting in plain sight?

     Of course, some investment strategies - for instance, our=20
efforts in arbitrage over the years - require wide diversification.=20
If significant risk exists in a single transaction, overall risk=20
should be reduced by making that purchase one of many mutually-
independent commitments.  Thus, you may consciously purchase a=20
risky investment - one that indeed has a significant possibility of=20
causing loss or injury - if you believe that your gain, weighted=20
for probabilities, considerably exceeds your loss, comparably=20
weighted, and if you can commit to a number of similar, but=20
unrelated opportunities.  Most venture capitalists employ this=20
strategy.  Should you choose to pursue this course, you should=20
adopt the outlook of the casino that owns a roulette wheel, which=20
will want to see lots of action because it is favored by=20
probabilities, but will refuse to accept a single, huge bet.

     Another situation requiring wide diversification occurs when=20
an investor who does not understand the economics of specific=20
businesses nevertheless believes it in his interest to be a long-
term owner of American industry.  That investor should both own a=20
large number of equities and space out his purchases.  By=20
periodically investing in an index fund, for example, the know-
nothing investor can actually out-perform most investment=20
professionals.  Paradoxically, when "dumb" money acknowledges its=20
limitations, it ceases to be dumb.

     On the other hand, if you are a know-<i>something</i> investor, able=
=20
to understand business economics and to find five to ten sensibly-
priced companies that possess important long-term competitive=20
advantages, conventional diversification makes no sense for you. =20
It is apt simply to hurt your results and increase your risk.  I=20
cannot understand why an investor of that sort elects to put money=20
into a business that is his 20th favorite rather than simply adding=20
that money to his top choices - the businesses he understands best=20
and that present the least risk, along with the greatest profit=20
potential.  In the words of the prophet Mae West:  "Too much of a=20
good thing can be wonderful."

<b>Corporate Governance</b>

     At our annual meetings, someone usually asks "What happens to=20
this place if you get hit by a truck?"  I'm glad they are still=20
asking the question in this form.  It won't be too long before the=20
query becomes:  "What happens to this place if you <i>don't</i> get hit by=
=20
a truck?"

     Such questions, in any event, raise a reason for me to discuss=20
corporate governance, a hot topic during the past year.  In=20
general, I believe that directors have stiffened their spines=20
recently and that shareholders are now being treated somewhat more=20
like true owners than was the case not long ago.  Commentators on=20
corporate governance, however, seldom make any distinction among=20
three fundamentally different manager/owner situations that exist=20
in publicly-held companies.  Though the legal responsibility of=20
directors is identical throughout, their ability to effect change=20
differs in each of the cases.  Attention usually falls on the first=20
case, because it prevails on the corporate scene.  Since Berkshire=20
falls into the second category, however, and will someday fall into=20
the third, we will discuss all three variations.

     The first, and by far most common, board situation is one in=20
which a corporation has no controlling shareholder.  In that case,=20
I believe directors should behave as if there is a single absentee=20
owner, whose long-term interest they should try to further in all=20
proper ways.  Unfortunately, "long-term" gives directors a lot of=20
wiggle room.  If they lack either integrity or the ability to think=20
independently, directors can do great violence to shareholders=20
while still claiming to be acting in their long-term interest.  But=20
assume the board is functioning well and must deal with a=20
management that is mediocre or worse.  Directors then have the=20
responsibility for changing that management, just as an intelligent=20
owner would do if he were present.  And if able but greedy managers=20
over-reach and try to dip too deeply into the shareholders'=20
pockets, directors must slap their hands.

     In this plain-vanilla case, a director who sees something he=20
doesn't like should attempt to persuade the other directors of his=20
views.  If he is successful, the board will have the muscle to make=20
the appropriate change.  Suppose, though, that the unhappy director=20
can't get other directors to agree with him.  He should then feel=20
free to make his views known to the absentee owners.  Directors=20
seldom do that, of course.  The temperament of many directors would=20
in fact be incompatible with critical behavior of that sort.  But I=20
see nothing improper in such actions, assuming the issues are=20
serious.  Naturally, the complaining director can expect a vigorous=20
rebuttal from the unpersuaded directors, a prospect that should=20
discourage the dissenter from pursuing trivial or non-rational=20
causes.

     For the boards just discussed, I believe the directors ought=20
to be relatively few in number - say, ten or less - and ought to=20
come mostly from the outside.  The outside board members should=20
establish standards for the CEO's performance and should also=20
periodically meet, without his being present, to evaluate his=20
performance against those standards.

     The requisites for board membership should be business savvy,=20
interest in the job, and owner-orientation.  Too often, directors=20
are selected simply because they are prominent or add diversity to=20
the board.  That practice is a mistake.  Furthermore, mistakes in=20
selecting directors are particularly serious because appointments=20
are so hard to undo:  The pleasant but vacuous director need never=20
worry about job security.

     The second case is that existing at Berkshire, where the=20
controlling owner is also the manager.  At some companies, this=20
arrangement is facilitated by the existence of two classes of stock=20
endowed with disproportionate voting power.  In these situations,=20
it's obvious that the board does not act as an agent between owners=20
and management and that the directors cannot effect change except=20
through persuasion.  Therefore, if the owner/manager is mediocre or=20
worse - or is over-reaching - there is little a director can do=20
about it except object.  If the directors having no connections to=20
the owner/manager make a unified argument, it may well have some=20
effect.  More likely it will not.

     If change does not come, and the matter is sufficiently=20
serious, the outside directors should resign.  Their resignation=20
will signal their doubts about management, and it will emphasize=20
that no outsider is in a position to correct the owner/manager's=20
shortcomings.

     The third governance case occurs when there is a controlling=20
owner who is not involved in management.  This case, examples of=20
which are Hershey Foods and Dow Jones, puts the outside directors=20
in a potentially useful position.  If they become unhappy with=20
either the competence or integrity of the manager, they can go=20
directly to the owner (who may also be on the board) and report=20
their dissatisfaction.  This situation is ideal for an outside=20
director, since he need make his case only to a single, presumably=20
interested owner, who can forthwith effect change if the argument=20
is persuasive.  Even so, the dissatisfied director has only that=20
single course of action.  If he remains unsatisfied about a=20
critical matter, he has no choice but to resign.

     Logically, the third case should be the most effective in=20
insuring first-class management.  In the second case the owner is=20
not going to fire himself, and in the first case, directors often=20
find it very difficult to deal with mediocrity or mild over-
reaching.  Unless the unhappy directors can win over a majority of=20
the board - an awkward social and logistical task, particularly if=20
management's behavior is merely odious, not egregious - their hands=20
are effectively tied.  In practice, directors trapped in situations=20
of this kind usually convince themselves that by staying around=20
they can do at least some good.  Meanwhile, management proceeds=20
unfettered.

     In the third case, the owner is neither judging himself nor=20
burdened with the problem of garnering a majority.  He can also=20
insure that outside directors are selected who will bring useful=20
qualities to the board.  These directors, in turn, will know that=20
the good advice they give will reach the right ears, rather than=20
being stifled by a recalcitrant management.  If the controlling=20
owner is intelligent and self-confident, he will make decisions in=20
respect to management that are meritocratic and pro-shareholder. =20
Moreover - and this is critically important - he can readily=20
correct any mistake he makes.

     At Berkshire we operate in the second mode now and will for as=20
long as I remain functional.  My health, let me add, is excellent.=20
For better or worse, you are likely to have me as an owner/manager=20
for some time.

     After my death, all of my stock will go to my wife, Susie,=20
should she survive me, or to a foundation if she dies before I do.=20
In neither case will taxes and bequests require the sale of=20
consequential amounts of stock.

     When my stock is transferred to either my wife or the=20
foundation, Berkshire will enter the third governance mode, going=20
forward with a vitally interested, but non-management, owner and=20
with a management that must perform for that owner.  In preparation=20
for that time, Susie was elected to the board a few years ago, and=20
in 1993 our son, Howard, joined the board.  These family members=20
will not be managers of the company in the future, but they will=20
represent the controlling interest should anything happen to me. =20
Most of our other directors are also significant owners of=20
Berkshire stock, and each has a strong owner-orientation.  All in=20
all, we're prepared for "the truck."

<b>Shareholder-Designated Contributions</b>

     About 97% of all eligible shares participated in Berkshire's=20
1993 shareholder-designated contributions program.  Contributions=20
made through the program were $9.4 million and 3,110 charities were=20
recipients.

     Berkshire's practice in respect to discretionary philanthropy=20
- as contrasted to its policies regarding contributions that are=20
clearly related to the company's business activities - differs=20
significantly from that of other publicly-held corporations. =20
There, most corporate contributions are made pursuant to the wishes=20
of the CEO (who often will be responding to social pressures),=20
employees (through matching gifts), or directors (through matching=20
gifts or requests they make of the CEO).

     At Berkshire, we believe that the company's money is the=20
owners' money, just as it would be in a closely-held corporation,=20
partnership, or sole proprietorship.  Therefore, if funds are to be=20
given to causes unrelated to Berkshire's business activities, it is=20
the charities favored by our owners that should receive them. =20
We've yet to find a CEO who believes he should personally fund the=20
charities favored by his shareholders.  Why, then, should they foot=20
the bill for his picks?

     Let me add that our program is easy to administer.  Last fall,=20
for two months, we borrowed one person from National Indemnity to=20
help us implement the instructions that came from our 7,500=20
registered shareholders.  I'd guess that the average corporate=20
program in which employee gifts are matched incurs far greater=20
administrative costs.  Indeed, our entire corporate overhead is=20
less than half the size of our charitable contributions.  <font size=3D"-1"=
><i>(Charlie,=20
however, insists that I tell you that $1.4 million of our $4.9 million over=
head is=20
attributable to our corporate jet, The Indefensible.)</i></font>

     Below is a list showing the largest categories to which our=20
shareholders have steered their contributions.

     (a) 347 churches and synagogues received 569 gifts
     (b) 283 colleges and universities received 670 gifts
     (c) 244 K-12 schools (about two-thirds secular, one-
         third religious) received 525 gifts
     (d) 288 institutions dedicated to art, culture or the=20
         humanities received 447 gifts
     (e) 180 religious social-service organizations (split=20
         about equally between Christian and Jewish) received=20
         411 gifts=20
     (f) 445 secular social-service organizations (about 40%=20
         youth-related) received 759 gifts
     (g) 153 hospitals received 261 gifts
     (h) 186 health-related organizations (American Heart=20
         Association, American Cancer Society, etc.) received=20
         320 gifts

     Three things about this list seem particularly interesting to=20
me.  First, to some degree it indicates what people choose to give=20
money to when they are acting of their own accord, free of pressure=20
from solicitors or emotional appeals from charities.  Second, the=20
contributions programs of publicly-held companies almost never=20
allow gifts to churches and synagogues, yet clearly these=20
institutions are what many shareholders would like to support. =20
Third, the gifts made by our shareholders display conflicting=20
philosophies:  130 gifts were directed to organizations that=20
believe in making abortions readily available for women and 30=20
gifts were directed to organizations (other than churches) that=20
discourage or are opposed to abortion.

     Last year I told you that I was thinking of raising the amount=20
that Berkshire shareholders can give under our designated-
contributions program and asked for your comments.  We received a=20
few well-written letters opposing the entire idea, on the grounds=20
that it was our job to run the business and not our job to force=20
shareholders into making charitable gifts.  Most of the=20
shareholders responding, however, noted the tax efficiency of the=20
plan and urged us to increase the designated amount.  Several=20
shareholders who have given stock to their children or=20
grandchildren told me that they consider the program a particularly=20
good way to get youngsters thinking at an early age about the=20
subject of giving.  These people, in other words, perceive the=20
program to be an educational, as well as philanthropic, tool.  The=20
bottom line is that we did raise the amount in 1993, from $8 per=20
share to $10.

     In addition to the shareholder-designated contributions that=20
Berkshire distributes, our operating businesses make contributions,=20
including merchandise, averaging about $2.5 million annually. =20
These contributions support local charities, such as The United=20
Way, and produce roughly commensurate benefits for our businesses.

     We suggest that new shareholders read the description of our=20
shareholder-designated contributions program that appears on pages=20
50-51.  <i>To participate in future programs, you must make sure your=20
shares are registered in the name of the actual owner, not in the=20
nominee name of a broker, bank or depository.  Shares not so=20
registered on August 31, 1994 will be ineligible for the 1994=20
program.</i>

<b>A Few Personal Items</b>

     Mrs. B - Rose Blumkin - had her 100th birthday on December 3,=20
1993.  (The candles cost more than the cake.)  That was a day on=20
which the store was scheduled to be open in the evening.  Mrs. B,=20
who works seven days a week, for however many hours the store=20
operates, found the proper decision quite obvious:  She simply=20
postponed her party until an evening when the store was closed.

     Mrs. B's story is well-known but worth telling again.  She=20
came to the United States 77 years ago, unable to speak English and=20
devoid of formal schooling.  In 1937, she founded the Nebraska=20
Furniture Mart with $500.  Last year the store had sales of $200=20
million, a larger amount by far than that recorded by any other=20
home furnishings store in the United States.  Our part in all of=20
this began ten years ago when Mrs. B sold control of the business=20
to Berkshire Hathaway, a deal we completed without obtaining=20
audited financial statements, checking real estate records, or=20
getting any warranties.  In short, her word was good enough for us.

     Naturally, I was delighted to attend Mrs. B's birthday party.=20
After all, she's promised to attend <i>my</i> 100th.

                     * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Katharine Graham retired last year as the chairman of The=20
Washington Post Company, having relinquished the CEO title three=20
years ago.  In 1973, we purchased our stock in her company for=20
about $10 million.  Our holding now garners $7 million a year in=20
dividends and is worth over $400 million.  At the time of our=20
purchase, we knew that the economic prospects of the company were=20
good.  But equally important, Charlie and I concluded that Kay=20
would prove to be an outstanding manager and would treat all=20
shareholders honorably.  That latter consideration was particularly=20
important because The Washington Post Company has two classes of=20
stock, a structure that we've seen some managers abuse.

     All of our judgments about this investment have been validated=20
by events.  Kay's skills as a manager were underscored this past=20
year when she was elected by <i>Fortune's</i> Board of Editors to the=20
Business Hall of Fame.  On behalf of our shareholders, Charlie and=20
I had long ago put her in Berkshire's Hall of Fame.

                     * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Another of last year's retirees was Don Keough of Coca-Cola,=20
although, as he puts it, his retirement lasted "about 14 hours." =20
Don is one of the most extraordinary human beings I've ever known -=20
a man of enormous business talent, but, even more important, a man=20
who brings out the absolute best in everyone lucky enough to=20
associate with him.  Coca-Cola wants its product to be present at=20
the happy times of a person's life.  Don Keough, as an individual,=20
invariably increases the happiness of those around him.  It's=20
impossible to think about Don without feeling good.

     I will edge up to how I met Don by slipping in a plug for my=20
neighborhood in Omaha:  Though Charlie has lived in California for=20
45 years, his home as a boy was about 200 feet away from the house=20
where I now live; my wife, Susie, grew up 1 1/2 blocks away; and we=20
have about 125 Berkshire shareholders in the zip code.  As for Don,=20
in 1958 he bought the house directly across the street from mine. =20
He was then a coffee salesman with a big family and a small income.

     The impressions I formed in those days about Don were a factor=20
in my decision to have Berkshire make a record $1 billion=20
investment in Coca-Cola in 1988-89.  Roberto Goizueta had become=20
CEO of Coke in 1981, with Don alongside as his partner.  The two of=20
them took hold of a company that had stagnated during the previous=20
decade and moved it from $4.4 billion of market value to $58=20
billion in less than 13 years.  What a difference a pair of=20
managers like this makes, even when their product has been around=20
for 100 years.

                      * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Frank Rooney did double duty last year.  In addition to=20
leading H. H. Brown to record profits - 35% above the 1992 high -=20
